# toolkit.render.gamma — sRGB <-> linear-light lookup tables for
# gamma-correct alpha blending.
#
# Every AA blend in this toolkit (toolkit.ui.interp's mix_color/
# mix_color_hq/mix3_color_hq) mixes fg/bg channel VALUES directly --
# `(fg*coverage + bg*(255-coverage)) / 255`. Those channel values are sRGB
# (gamma-encoded) bytes, the same encoding every #hex colour in this
# toolkit is already written in, but a straight weighted average of two
# sRGB bytes is NOT the same colour a human sees when the two colours are
# actually blended, because sRGB is a non-linear (roughly gamma-2.2)
# encoding of light intensity. Averaging in the ENCODED space biases every
# blended edge pixel toward the darker of the two colours (a well-known,
# named rendering bug: naive alpha blending on sRGB data reads as slightly
# "muddy" or "dirty" compared to real vector renderers, which blend in
# LINEAR light and re-encode -- exactly what real-time engines and
# libraries like Skia do, and one concrete, measurable reason nebula's
# rounded-corner edges looked less crisp than a browser's, independent of
# antialiasing RESOLUTION (see analytic_coverage's own comment for the
# resolution half of that gap, already fixed).
#
# No floating point, no runtime pow(): both directions are 256-entry
# integer lookup tables, baked ONCE (see the generation formula below) and
# committed as plain data, the same "compute expensive things offline,
# ship only the result" approach toolkit.text.font_data's baked glyph
# atlas already uses. 512 bytes total -- negligible even on the
# bare-metal/UEFI tiers this toolkit also builds for, and correctness
# there costs nothing extra since it's pure table lookup.
#
# Generated by evaluating the precise sRGB<->linear transfer function
# (IEC 61966-2-1) at each of the 256 byte values in double precision and
# rounding to the nearest byte -- not a flat gamma-2.2 approximation,
# though the difference is only visible at the very low end:
#   srgb_to_linear(c): c<=0.04045 ? c/12.92 : ((c+0.055)/1.055)^2.4
#   linear_to_srgb(c): c<=0.0031308 ? c*12.92 : 1.055*c^(1/2.4)-0.055
# (c normalized to 0..1 first, result scaled back to 0..255 and rounded)
#
# NOTE (real compiler gap, found building this): a module-level `pub mut`
# must be declared TEXTUALLY BEFORE any function in the same file that
# references it, or name resolution fails with "not defined" -- misreported
# against the IMPORTING file's line numbers, not this one, which cost real
# time to trace back here. Confirmed with a two-line repro (swap the
# declaration order of a `pub mut` and the function reading it in the same
# file). Worked around by declaring both tables first; not fixed in the
# compiler this session.
#
# ## Fixed-size arrays, not List[int] (2026-09-09)
#
# These were originally `pub mut X: List[int] = [...]` array literals.
# Building nebula's wasm (--freestanding) target surfaced a real, deep
# compiler bug: EVERY `pub export def` gets a compiler-injected "run once"
# guard that initializes ALL reachable module-level globals the first time
# ANY exported function is called (freestanding has no libc/_start to run
# them the normal way) -- and List[int] literal init allocates on the
# heap via the user's @allocator/@realloc hooks. In render_web.tr, THE
# call that sets up the allocator (arena_init, inside nebula_heap_init)
# is itself a `pub export def` subject to that SAME guard, and the guard's
# global-init code runs BEFORE that function's own body -- so the very
# first allocation these two tables need happens before the arena has been
# told its base/size, and the pointer-arithmetic bootstrap check
# (_next=_end=_base=0) can't catch it: it crashes with a real
# out-of-bounds memory access in the browser instead. Confirmed by
# tracing the generated C (`build/main.c`): the two `List_i64_append(...)`
# chains for these tables ran, then only afterward did `arena_init(base,
# size)` execute in the same function.
#
# A general compiler fix would need EITHER a new "static-backed, never
# reallocated" List representation (real scope: List_i64's struct is one
# of the most fundamental, pervasively-used types in the whole runtime --
# changing its layout is a high-blast-radius change, not a contained one)
# or a separate compiler-internal bootstrap allocator independent of the
# user's own arena (a new runtime concept). Both are real future work, not
# attempted here. Fixed-size arrays sidestep the entire problem instead:
# `[int; 256]` is an inline VALUE type (no heap pointer at all), so
# `_init_gamma_tables()`'s 512 plain element writes below need no
# allocator, no arena, and no ordering relative to anything else --
# correct on every target uniformly, not just freestanding. Explicit lazy
# init on first use (`_gamma_tables_ready`) rather than relying on any
# compiler-provided global-init mechanism at all, so this works exactly
# the same way regardless of whether that mechanism runs, when, or on
# which target.

pub mut SRGB_TO_LINEAR: [int; 256]
pub mut LINEAR_TO_SRGB: [int; 256]
mut _gamma_tables_ready: bool = false

def _init_gamma_tables() -> void:
    SRGB_TO_LINEAR[0] = 0
    SRGB_TO_LINEAR[1] = 0
